1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence is moving from systems that primarily generate information toward systems that can observe environments, plan, call tools, execute code, interact with external services, maintain state, coordinate with other agents, and pursue objectives over extended periods. NIST describes AI agents as systems capable of autonomous actions affecting real-world systems and environments. Its 2026 AI Agent Standards Initiative focuses on secure deployment, interoperability, identity, and authorization, identifying six essential standards: OAuth 2.0/2.1, OpenID Connect, SPIFFE/SPIRE, SCIM, NGAC, and the Model Context Protocol.

This transition changes the security problem. The relevant object is no longer simply a model. It is a closed-loop socio-technical system.

We define an agentic system as:

\mathcal{A}_i(t) = (I_i, M_i, S_i, P_i(t), T_i(t), R_i(t), \mathcal{G}_i)

where $I_i$ is the cryptographic identity, $M_i$ the computational model, $S_i$ the persistent state, $P_i(t)$ the authority vector, $T_i(t)$ the set of accessible tools, $R_i(t)$ the allocated resources, and $\mathcal{G}_i$ the set of assigned guardians.

The central thesis of this paper is that safety cannot be reduced to improving $M_i$. A highly capable model placed inside an insecure authority architecture can remain dangerous even if the model itself is extensively aligned.

