2. The Fundamental Distinction: Intelligence Is Not Authority

The framework begins with a distinction often obscured in AI safety discussions:

\boxed{\text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority}}

An agent may possess substantial reasoning or planning capability without possessing authority to modify external systems. Conversely, a relatively modest model can become highly consequential if it receives broad permissions. OWASP identifies this as Excessive Agency, with three recurring causes: excessive functionality, excessive permissions, and excessive autonomy.

The corresponding security invariant is:

\text{MaxConsequence}(\mathcal{A}_i) \leq f\!\left(P_i(t)\right) + \epsilon_{\text{unknown}}

where $f$ is bounded by the authority envelope and $\epsilon_{\text{unknown}}$ is a residual term for unknown unknowns. The engineering objective is to make $\epsilon_{\text{unknown}}$ small and measurable, not to assume it is zero.

