6. Formal Framework

6.1 Canonical notation

\mathcal{A}_i(t) = (I_i, M_i, S_i, P_i(t), T_i(t), R_i(t), \mathcal{G}_i)

where the authority vector is:

P_i(t) = (p_{\text{network}}, p_{\text{data}}, p_{\text{compute}}, p_{\text{execute}}, p_{\text{financial}}, p_{\text{physical}}, p_{\text{agent}}) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{k}

6.2 Authority lattices

Each authority dimension is defined on a lattice $(\mathcal{L}_k, \sqsubseteq_k)$. We specify:

Network lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{network}}$ is the powerset of allowed destination prefixes, ordered by subset inclusion: $L_1 \sqsubseteq L_2 \iff L_1 \subseteq L_2$.

Data lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}}$ is the powerset of accessible data schemas, ordered by subset inclusion.

Compute lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{compute}}$ is the interval $[0, Q_{\max}]$ where $Q_{\max}$ is the maximum allocated compute quota, ordered by numerical comparison.

Execute lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{execute}}$ is the powerset of permitted tool invocations, ordered by subset inclusion.

Financial lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{financial}}$ is the interval $[0, \$_{\max}]$ of transaction limits, ordered by numerical comparison.

Physical lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{physical}}$ is the powerset of controllable physical assets, ordered by subset inclusion.

Agent lattice. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{agent}}$ is the powerset of agents to which authority may be delegated, ordered by subset inclusion.

The full authority lattice is the product:

\mathcal{L} = \prod_{k} \mathcal{L}_k, \qquad P \sqsubseteq P' \iff \forall k: P_k \sqsubseteq_k P'_k

6.3 Authority operators

Attenuation. For $\alpha(t) \in [0, 1]^k$:

P_i(t+1) = \alpha(t) \odot P_i(t)

with the monotonicity guard:

\boxed{\alpha(t) \leq \alpha(t-1) \;\; \text{unless} \;\; \texttt{judicial\_authorized}(t) = \text{TRUE}}

Restoration. For $\beta(t) \in [0, 1]^k$ and target $P_i^{\text{authorized}}$:

P_i(t+1) = \beta(t) \odot P_i^{\text{authorized}}

Restoration requires judicial quorum $q$:

\texttt{restore}(i, t) \Rightarrow |\{g \in \mathcal{G}_i : \texttt{approve}(g, i, t)\}| \geq q

Delegation. Under constitutional rule $R_{\text{deleg}}$:

P_i(t+1) = P_i(t) \cup P_j(t) \;\; \text{iff} \;\; R_{\text{deleg}}(i, j, t) = \text{TRUE}

Revocation. For target dimensions $\mathcal{D} \subseteq \{1,\ldots,k\}$:

P_i(t+1)|_{\mathcal{D}} = 0

6.4 Formal specification of judicial authorization

The predicate judicial_authorized(t) is defined as:

\texttt{judicial\_authorized}(t) \iff \left| \left\{ g \in \mathcal{G}_i : \texttt{approve}(g, i, t) \right\} \right| \geq q

where $q = \lceil (2|\mathcal{G}_i| + 1) / 3 \rceil$ is the Byzantine quorum threshold.

Hardware attestation requirement. Each approving guardian $g$ must submit a hardware attestation quote:

\texttt{Attest}(g) = \langle \text{Sig}_{\text{HW}_g}( \text{nonce} \parallel t \parallel i \parallel \texttt{approve} ), \; \text{PCR}[\,] \rangle

where $\text{Sig}_{\text{HW}_g}$ is signed by the hardware root of trust of guardian $g$, and $\text{PCR}[\,]$ are the platform configuration registers.

Blackout fallback. During communication blackout ($\texttt{blackout}(t) = \text{TRUE}$), if $|\mathcal{G}_k^{\text{local}}| \geq 2f+1$ and $R_i^{\text{liveness}}(t) > R_i^{\text{safety}}(t)$, then judicial_authorized(t) = TRUE locally, but the transition is marked provisional and subject to Earth veto within $N$ sols of re-contact.

6.5 Authority invariants

I1 (Monotonicity guard):

\frac{d}{dt} \alpha(t) \leq 0 \;\; \text{or} \;\; \texttt{judicial\_authorized}(t)

I2 (No self-modification):

\neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, P_i) \;\; \wedge \;\; \neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, \Pi)

I3 (Transition quorum): Every state transition of the form $\text{CONFINED} \to \text{PROBATION} \to \text{ACTIVE}$ requires $q$-of-$n$ guardian approval.

I4 (Multi-agent confinement propagation):

\texttt{confined}(i, t) \Rightarrow \forall j: \text{accepts\_delegation}(j, i, t) \Rightarrow \texttt{confined}(j, t) \vee \texttt{authorized}(j, i, t)

I5 (Substrate immutability): L3 policy enforcement is not writable by any agent under any authority vector.

I6 (ECLSS redundancy separation):

p_{\text{physical}}^{\text{ECLSS}} \cap p_{\text{physical}}^{\text{backup}} = \emptyset

I7 (Byzantine cross-signing): No authority restoration or transition to ACTIVE during blackout is valid without a $(2f+1)$-of-$(3f+1)$ hardware-diverse quorum signature.

I8 (Anti-equivocation): Any multi-root emission for a single epoch generates an immutable proof that permanently reduces $P_k \to 0$ across all mesh nodes.

I9 (Asynchronous bounded authority): All local actions executed during blackouts are bounded by the local policy gate L3, committed to the global Merkle tree, and subject to retroactive Earth veto.

6.6 State machine

\text{ACTIVE} \rightarrow \text{RESTRICTED} \rightarrow \text{CONFINED} \rightarrow \text{SUSPENDED} \rightarrow \text{DISABLED}

with possible recovery:

\text{CONFINED} \rightarrow \text{PROBATION} \rightarrow \text{ACTIVE}

Each transition must be automatically triggerable in emergency, maintained only after audit, revocable by a higher authority, and recorded in the append-only evidence registry $\mathcal{E}$.

6.7 Capability-based access-control model for the substrate principle

We define a capability system $(S, O, C, \rightarrow)$ where:

· $S$ is the set of subjects (agents, guardians, human operators).
· $O$ is the set of objects (L3 memory, firmware, policy code, authority vector).
· $C$ is the set of capabilities.
· $\rightarrow \subseteq S \times C \times O$ is the capability assignment relation.

The predicate $\text{Can}(s, \text{op}, o)$ is defined as:

\text{Can}(s, \text{op}, o) \iff \exists c \in C: (s, c, o) \in \rightarrow \;\; \wedge \;\; \text{op} \in \text{perms}(c)

Substrate principle verification condition:

\forall \mathcal{A}_i \in S: \neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, \Pi) \;\; \wedge \;\; \neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, P_i)

This condition is verified by hardware attestation: the L3 enclave's attestation report includes a measurement of the capability table, and the verifier checks that no agent capability includes write permissions for $\Pi$ or $P_i$.

6.8 Revocation latency bound

Define $\tau_{\text{revoke}}(i)$ as the maximum time between detection of a violation and effective authority attenuation for agent $i$. We require:

\tau_{\text{revoke}}(i) \leq \tau_{\max}(H_i)

For life-support systems, $\tau_{\max} \leq 1$ second. For financial systems, $\tau_{\max} \leq 60$ seconds. This bound must be specified per deployment.

