7. Seven-Layer Architecture

L_1 \rightarrow L_2 \rightarrow L_3 \rightarrow L_4 \rightarrow L_5 \rightarrow L_6 \rightarrow L_7

L1 — Identity. Every agent receives a unique cryptographic identity $I_i$, persistent for accountability but revocable to prevent indefinite authority.

L2 — Authority. Each agent possesses a formal authority vector $P_i(t)$ defined on the lattice $\mathcal{L}$.

L3 — Policy Enforcement. Every consequential action passes through:

a_i \rightarrow \Pi(a_i, \text{context}) \rightarrow \{\text{ALLOW}, \text{DENY}, \text{ESCALATE}\}

L3 is immutable by agents (Substrate Principle) and executes within a hardware-isolated enclave.

L4 — Runtime Observation. The system continuously observes tool calls, network destinations, permission requests, execution traces, resource consumption, communication patterns, state transitions, and attempts to modify security controls.

L5 — Independent Oversight. A separate monitoring system evaluates the agent:

\boxed{\text{Agent} \neq \text{Monitor}}

L6 — Containment. If risk exceeds threshold $R_i(t) > \tau$, the system progressively attenuates authority.

L7 — Governance. Final high-consequence decisions separated from automated detection:

\boxed{\text{Detection} \neq \text{Adjudication} \neq \text{Enforcement} \neq \text{Governance}}

