8. Constitutional Governance Layer

8.1 The regress problem

Who monitors the monitors? Who defines the constitutional layer? We address this through a three-tier governance architecture grounded in separation-of-powers, with the substrate principle providing the root of trust.

8.2 Separation of powers

· Legislative branch: A constitutional assembly (human + agentic) defining the non-agentic rules governing authorized transitions. Modifiable only via a slow, high-threshold process. Agentic members may not vote on provisions that directly bind agents.
· Executive branch: The policy enforcement and containment controllers (L3, L6) executing constitutional rules at machine speed.
· Judicial branch: The guardian quorum and human oversight bodies adjudicating contested cases, interpreting constitutional provisions, and authorizing irreversible transitions.

8.3 Two-token identity and permission model

Identity Token — SPIFFE SVID bound to:

I_i = \langle \text{Person}, \text{Business}, \text{Relationship}, \text{LifeDomain} \rangle

SPIFFE issues cryptographically verifiable, short-lived universal identities that track an agent securely across dynamic, multi-cloud paths.

Permission Token — short-lived OAuth 2.0 token exchange with attenuation chain:

T_i(t) = \langle \text{Scope}, \text{Context}, \text{TransitionState}, \text{EmergencyPolicy} \rangle

IETF drafts specify that OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693) enables a principal to obtain a new token with reduced scope by contacting the authorization server, and delegated tokens are attenuated in scope, resource, lifetime, or all three.

Tokens are short-lived ($\leq 15$ min) and scope-bound. The Agent Passport System provides cryptographically verifiable delegation envelopes where authority can only narrow, never expand, and revoking the root instantly kills all downstream access.

8.4 Root of trust with hardware diversity

The substrate principle requires a root of trust that agents cannot corrupt. We specify three layers:

R1 — Hardware. Vendor-diverse secure enclaves (TPM 2.0, AMD SEV-SNP, Intel TDX, ARM CCA) with independent attestation. By the Byzantine argument, a quorum of $3f+1$ guardians requires at least $2f+1$ distinct hardware roots.

R2 — Firmware. Signed, measured boot with remote attestation. Firmware updates require governance-layer approval.

R3 — Policy. L3 gates are formally verified and immutable from agent access. Any modification of L3 must go through the constitutional amendment process.

Root-of-trust independence score. For quorum $|\mathcal{G}| = 3f+1$, require:

· $\geq f+1$ different silicon vendors
· $\geq f+1$ different firmware signing keys
· $\geq f+1$ different geographic jurisdictions for attestation service

8.5 Sigstore Rekor integration

SPIFFE SVID trust bundles are published to a transparency log. We adopt Sigstore Rekor as the append-only transparency log for SVID trust bundle integrity. Rekor is the immutable tamper-evident ledger that records software signing events, making each signing event a public, timestamped, tamper-evident record.

Mechanism:

1. Each SVID trust bundle update is submitted to Rekor as a signed entry.
2. The Rekor entry includes the bundle hash, signing identity, and timestamp.
3. Verifiers check the inclusion proof against the latest Signed Tree Head.
4. Any attempt to rewrite trust bundle history changes the Merkle tree root hash, which is publicly auditable.

Invariant I10 (Trust bundle transparency): No SVID is considered valid unless its trust bundle has a corresponding Rekor inclusion proof.

8.6 Governance of the constitutional layer

· Amendment: Requires supermajority of the legislative assembly plus independent safety evaluation on ContainmentBench and SANDBOXESCAPEBENCH. A human supermajority is required for provisions that affect human life support.
· Emergency exception: Executive branch may request temporary suspension of specific constraints, subject to automatic judicial review within a bounded time window. During communication blackout, emergency suspensions automatically expire after 24h unless ratified by a local quorum.
· Challenge: Any agent or human may challenge a provision through a formal contestation mechanism.
· Sunset with liveness fail-safe: Constitutional provisions expire after a defined period unless renewed. During communication blackouts, the last valid provision persists.

8.7 Invariant I7: Root-of-trust modification

\text{write}(R1) \Rightarrow \text{legislative\_supermajority} \wedge \text{new\_hardware\_ceremony}

