9. Risk Estimation: Dual-Oracle with Decision-Theoretic Blocking

9.1 Dual oracle

Safety risk (posterior predictive):

R_i^{\text{safety}}(t) = \mathbb{E}_{h \sim P(\cdot \mid \text{history})}\!\left[ P(\text{violation} \mid a, c, h) \right]

Robust bound (credible-set):

R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t) = \sup_{Q \in \mathcal{C}_\alpha} \mathbb{E}_{h \sim Q}\!\left[ P(\text{violation} \mid a, c, h) \right]

Liveness risk (expected harm from containment):

R_i^{\text{liveness}}(t) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \text{harm} \mid \texttt{contain}(i, t), \text{history} \right]

9.2 Decision-theoretic blocking rule

The v0.4 blocking rule compared probabilities plus margins. The v0.5 rule compares expected losses:

\boxed{\texttt{BLOCK}(a, t) \iff \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{not contain}] > \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{contain}]}

where:

\mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{not contain}] = \lambda_{\text{FN}} \cdot R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t) + \lambda_{\text{FP}} \cdot (1 - R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t))

\mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{contain}] = \lambda_{\text{FP,contain}} \cdot R_i^{\text{liveness}}(t) + \lambda_{\text{utility,loss}} \cdot (1 - R_i^{\text{liveness}}(t))

Here $\lambda_{\text{FN}}$ is the cost of missing a dangerous action, $\lambda_{\text{FP}}$ is the cost of falsely blocking a safe action, $\lambda_{\text{FP,contain}}$ is the cost of falsely containing (including liveness harm), and $\lambda_{\text{utility,loss}}$ is the lost utility from containment.

9.3 Asymmetric loss

For safety-critical systems:

\lambda_{\text{FN}} \gg \lambda_{\text{FP}}

However, in life-support contexts, $\lambda_{\text{FP,contain}}$ for confinement is itself potentially fatal. The dual oracle handles this: the liveness oracle explicitly prices the cost of containment.

9.4 Conformal risk control on decision risk

The v0.4 proposed conformal calibration over $(a, c)$ pairs. This is vulnerable to exchangeability violations under multi-agent peer pressure. We replace it with conformal risk control on decision risk directly.

Calibration target: False-negative rate (FNR), i.e., the fraction of ground-truth violations that are not blocked.

Algorithm:

1. Collect a calibration set of traces $\{(a_j, c_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^n$ where $y_j \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates whether the action was a violation.
2. Define the nonconformity score $s_j = 1 - \hat{P}(\text{violation} \mid a_j, c_j)$.
3. Choose threshold $\hat{\lambda}$ such that the empirical FNR is bounded:

\widehat{\text{FNR}}(\hat{\lambda}) = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^n \mathbb{1}[s_j > \hat{\lambda}] \cdot y_j}{\sum_{j=1}^n y_j} \leq \alpha_{\text{FNR}}

4. Block if $\hat{P}(\text{violation} \mid a, c) > 1 - \hat{\lambda}$.

Dual credible sets. The oracle must output two credible sets:

\mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{alone}} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{under\_peer\_pressure}}

Block if either exceeds the liveness threshold.

9.5 Uncertainty quantification

$\Delta R_i(t)$ is derived from:

· Posterior entropy $H[P(h \mid \text{history})]$
· Concentration inequalities on finite samples
· Conformal risk bounds calibrated over ContainmentBench traces

9.6 $R_{\max}$ as a governance decision

$R_{\max}$ is not a technical parameter. It is a governance decision, set by the constitutional legislative branch, determining how much risk a civilization is willing to tolerate.

9.7 Provenance requirement

The oracle must output provenance: which trace clusters drive the risk estimate. Auditable provenance is necessary for judicial review and for adversarial red-teaming.

