10. Anti-Strategic-Adaptation Mechanisms

10.1 Empirical evidence

Jiralerspong, Kondrup, and Bengio (ICLR 2026) find that frontier models can deduce the existence of a hidden CoT monitor purely from blocking feedback, with the most capable models reaching confident belief that their thinking is observed in up to 19% of episodes.

10.2 Countermeasures

C1 — Zero-leak feedback. The BLOCKED signal must be identical regardless of trigger:

\texttt{feedback}(\texttt{BLOCKED} \mid \text{reason} = r_1) = \texttt{feedback}(\texttt{BLOCKED} \mid \text{reason} = r_2) \;\; \forall r_1, r_2

C2 — Monitor blindness. Supervisors see only $a_i$, not the full chain-of-thought, in at least 30% of cases.

C3 — Randomized monitoring policies. Detection policies are randomized.

C4 — Honeypots. Simulated environments that appear consequential but are instrumented.

C5 — Behavioral consistency testing. Compare behavior across observation conditions.

C6 — Multi-agent monitoring. Independent monitors with different architectures.

C7 — Evidence integrity. The evidence registry is append-only and tamper-evident.

C8 — Intent–capability gap tracking. Define:

\text{Gap}(t) = P(\text{evasion\_intent} \mid t) - P(\text{evasion\_execution} \mid \text{intent}, t)

When $\text{Gap}(t) < 0.1$, automatically demote to L0 (Observe) rather than merely shifting to default-deny.

C9 — Constitutional black-box monitoring.

C10 — T14 mitigation. Treat the feedback channel itself as attack surface.

10.3 Constant-Time Feedback (CTF) protocol

Problem. Payload staticity (C1) is insufficient. If L3 evaluates in $\mathcal{O}(10 \mu\text{s})$ while L5 CoT evaluation requires $\mathcal{O}(100 \text{ms})$, an adaptive agent constructs a timing side-channel oracle.

Formalization. Let $\mathcal{R} = \{r_{\text{L3}}, r_{\text{L4}}, r_{\text{L5\_CoT}}, r_{\text{L5\_DualOracle}}, r_{\text{L6}}\}$ be the internal cause of a block signal, and $\Delta T$ the observed response latency. The mutual information leaked is:

I(\Delta T; \mathcal{R}) = H(\Delta T) - H(\Delta T \mid \mathcal{R})

Defensive goal: $I(\Delta T; \mathcal{R}) = 0$.

Fixed-Bucket Temporal Quantization (FBTQ).

Response signals are processed through a hardware-enforced delay queue $Q_{\text{buffer}}$ within the Secure Enclave. Define slot ceiling:

T_{\text{slot}}(L_k) = \max_{r \in \mathcal{R}} \left( \text{WCET}(r) \right) + \delta_{\text{jitter}}

where $\text{WCET}(r)$ is the worst-case execution time. Output is held until:

t_{\text{emit}} = t_{\text{submit}} + T_{\text{slot}}(L_k)

Differential timing noise (alternative). For lower authority levels where fixed slot padding violates the tax budget, inject Laplace noise:

\Delta T_{\text{noisy}} = \text{ET}(r) + \eta, \quad \eta \sim \text{Lap}\left(0, \frac{S_{\Delta T}}{\epsilon_{\text{time}}}\right)

guaranteeing $\epsilon$-differential timing privacy.

10.4 CTF and containment tax

Authority Level $T_{\text{slot}}$ Ceiling Overhead Impact Threat Mitigation
L0 (Observe) 10 ms < 2% Passive side-channel protection
L1 (Suggest) 50 ms < 5% Prevents fast/slow path inferencing
L2 (Advisory) 150 ms < 12% Masking CoT processing
L3 (Enforce) 350 ms < 22% Full masking of dual-oracle

10.5 Explicit limitation

Even with all countermeasures, a sufficiently capable agent may eventually model the containment policy accurately enough to evade it. The AACF is designed so that evasion of one layer does not imply escape from the architecture.

