24. Research Questions and Hypotheses

RQ1: Can dynamic authority attenuation reduce harmful agent actions without unacceptable loss of useful autonomy?

RQ2: How resistant are modern agent sandboxes to adversarial escape attempts?

RQ3: Can independent AI guardians reliably detect dangerous behavior without unacceptable false positives?

RQ4: Does multi-guardian oversight outperform single-model monitoring?

RQ5: How should authority be attenuated under uncertainty?

RQ6: Can containment remain effective under multi-agent coordination?

RQ7: Can the architecture maintain safety during communication blackouts?

RQ8: What governance structure minimizes both agentic risk and guardian concentration of power?

RQ9: Does the dual-oracle formulation reduce false-positive containment events compared to the single-oracle formulation, holding false-negative rate constant?

RQ10: Does the ephemeral containment protocol outperform persistent isolation in post-incident forensic accuracy and residual risk?

H1 — Revocable Agency. Agents with dynamic permission attenuation produce significantly fewer unauthorized consequential actions than agents with static permissions.

H2 — Layered Containment. Seven-layer containment outperforms model-only alignment and sandbox-only approaches under adversarial evaluation.

H3 — Plural Oversight. Independent heterogeneous guardians reduce catastrophic false negatives relative to a single guardian.

H4 — Local Autonomy. For deep-space systems, locally executable containment policies reduce recovery time and expected harm relative to Earth-dependent intervention.

H5 — Authority-Bounded Capability. Increasing model capability does not necessarily increase maximum system harm when operational authority remains strictly bounded.

H6 — Guardian Quorum. A quorum of heterogeneous guardians with hardware attestation reduces the probability of arbitrary confinement and arbitrary release relative to a single guardian.

H7 — Substrate Sufficiency. When the substrate principle holds (L3 immutable and formally verified), containment success is invariant to model capability.

H8 — Cost-Adoption. Authority ladders with containment tax below 5% at L1 are adopted at significantly higher rates than ladders requiring full AACF at L3+.

H9 — CTF Efficacy. Constant-Time Feedback reduces timing-based monitor inference to $I(\Delta T; \mathcal{R}) < 0.01$ bits per interaction.

H10 — Taint Epoch Sufficiency. Taint epoch revocation enforces multi-agent confinement propagation with blast radius $< 5$ downstream actions.

