A.2 Theorem: Substrate Principle Sufficiency

Theorem A.2. If verification conditions V1–V4 hold, then containment does not depend on solving corrigibility.

Proof.

Step 1. Assume V1–V4 hold:

· V1: L3 executes in hardware-isolated enclave
· V2: Firmware is signed and measured with remote attestation
· V3: L3 policy code is formally verified and immutable
· V4: No agent capability permits write access to L3

Step 2. Containment requires the ability to attenuate authority: $P_i(t+1) = \alpha(t) \odot P_i(t)$ with $\alpha(t) \leq \alpha(t-1)$.

Step 3. By V4, no agent can write to $P_i$ directly. By V1–V3, no agent can write to $\Pi$, which computes $\alpha(t)$.

Step 4. Therefore, the containment controller (L6) can always attenuate authority regardless of the agent's internal state, goals, or willingness to cooperate.

Step 5. Corrigibility (Soares et al., 2014) is the property that an agent will not resist correction. The substrate principle makes containment independent of this property: even a non-corrigible agent cannot prevent attenuation because it lacks write access to the substrate.

Step 6. Yampolskiy (2012) showed that if $M_i$ can modify $\Pi$, containment is already lost. Contrapositively, if $M_i$ cannot modify $\Pi$, containment is preserved regardless of $M_i$'s internal properties.

\boxed{\text{Containment is a property of the substrate, not the agent.} \quad \blacksquare}

