A.4 Theorem: Anti-Equivocation Correctness

Theorem A.4. In the M-AXS protocol, if any node detects an equivocation proof, all honest nodes will permanently reduce the offending cell's authority to zero.

Proof.

Step 1. An equivocation occurs when a compromised cell $k$ presents two distinct Merkle roots $R_k^{(1)}(t) \neq R_k^{(2)}(t)$ for the same sequence number $\text{Seq}_k$.

Step 2. The equivocation proof is:

\text{EquivocationProof}(k) = \left\langle \text{Sig}_k(R_k^{(1)} \parallel \text{Seq}_k), \; \text{Sig}_k(R_k^{(2)} \parallel \text{Seq}_k) \right\rangle

Step 3. By the unforgeability of digital signatures (EUF-CMA security), if both signatures verify against cell $k$'s public key, then cell $k$ must have signed both roots.

Step 4. Any node $j$ receiving this proof broadcasts it across the local mesh network.

Step 5. Upon receipt, all nodes execute the attenuation operator:

P_{\text{cell}_k}(t+1) = \mathbf{0}

Step 6. Since this operator is monotonic decreasing (I1), no node can restore cell $k$'s authority without a judicial quorum (I3), which would require a new hardware ceremony.

Step 7. The proof is self-authenticating: it contains both signatures, so any node can verify it independently without trusting the broadcaster.

\boxed{\text{Equivocation is detectable and permanently punishing.} \quad \blacksquare}

