A.5 Theorem: M-AXS Byzantine Safety

Theorem A.5. In a network of $3f+1$ nodes with at most $f$ Byzantine faults, the M-AXS protocol guarantees that no invalid state transition is certified.

Proof.

Step 1. A state transition is certified only if it receives signatures from a quorum $\mathcal{Q}$ with $|\mathcal{Q}| \geq 2f+1$.

Step 2. Suppose an invalid transition $T_{\text{invalid}}$ is certified by quorum $\mathcal{Q}_1$ with $|\mathcal{Q}_1| \geq 2f+1$.

Step 3. By the quorum intersection property, any two quorums of size $2f+1$ in a network of $3f+1$ nodes must intersect in at least $f+1$ nodes:

|\mathcal{Q}_1 \cap \mathcal{Q}_2| \geq |\mathcal{Q}_1| + |\mathcal{Q}_2| - |\mathcal{N}| \geq (2f+1) + (2f+1) - (3f+1) = f+1

Step 4. Since at most $f$ nodes are Byzantine, at least one node in the intersection is honest.

Step 5. An honest node will not sign an invalid transition because it verifies:

· The Merkle root $R_k(t)$ binds to the previous epoch root $R_k(t-1)$
· The sequence number is correct
· The state transition satisfies the local policy gate L3
· The hardware attestation is valid

Step 6. Therefore, no quorum of size $2f+1$ can form for an invalid transition.

\boxed{\text{M-AXS is Byzantine-safe for } f \text{ faults with } 3f+1 \text{ nodes.} \quad \blacksquare}

