A.6 Theorem: Taint Epoch Sufficiency

Theorem A.6. Taint epoch revocation enforces invariant I4 (multi-agent confinement propagation) with blast radius bounded by the number of actions executed within the taint window.

Proof.

Step 1. Invariant I4 states:

\texttt{confined}(i, t) \Rightarrow \forall j: \text{accepts\_delegation}(j, i, t) \Rightarrow \texttt{confined}(j, t) \vee \texttt{authorized}(j, i, t)

Step 2. Without taint epoch revocation, agent $j$ might have accepted delegation from $i$ before confinement and continue using tokens issued by $i$.

Step 3. Taint epoch revocation, upon confined(i, t) becoming TRUE, revokes all tokens issued to $i$ in the last $T_{\text{taint}}$ window (default 15 min = token lifetime).

Step 4. By OAuth token introspection (RFC 7662), any resource server receiving a token can query the authorization server to check its validity.

Step 5. After revocation, any token issued by $i$ returns active: false from introspection. Agent $j$ cannot use the delegated authority because the token is invalid.

Step 6. Therefore, even if $j$ accepted delegation before confinement, the delegation becomes unusable immediately after revocation.

Step 7. The blast radius is bounded by the number of actions executed by $j$ using $i$'s tokens before revocation took effect. Since token lifetime is $\leq 15$ min and revocation is near-instantaneous ($\tau_{\text{revoke}} \leq 1$s for life-support), blast radius is bounded.

\boxed{\text{Taint epoch revocation enforces I4 with bounded blast radius.} \quad \blacksquare}

