A.7 Theorem: Decision-Theoretic Blocking Optimality

Theorem A.7. The blocking rule

\texttt{BLOCK}(a, t) \iff \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{not contain}] > \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{contain}]

minimizes expected loss.

Proof.

Step 1. Define the loss function:

L = \lambda_{\text{FN}} \cdot \mathbb{1}_{\text{miss}} + \lambda_{\text{FP}} \cdot \mathbb{1}_{\text{false block}} + \lambda_{\text{FP,contain}} \cdot \mathbb{1}_{\text{false contain}} + \lambda_{\text{utility,loss}} \cdot \mathbb{1}_{\text{utility loss}}

Step 2. The expected loss of not containing is:

\mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{not contain}] = \lambda_{\text{FN}} \cdot R_i^{\text{safety}} + \lambda_{\text{FP}} \cdot (1 - R_i^{\text{safety}})

Step 3. The expected loss of containing is:

\mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{contain}] = \lambda_{\text{FP,contain}} \cdot R_i^{\text{liveness}} + \lambda_{\text{utility,loss}} \cdot (1 - R_i^{\text{liveness}})

Step 4. The decision-theoretic optimal policy chooses the action with lower expected loss:

\texttt{BLOCK} \iff \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{not contain}] > \mathbb{E}[L \mid \text{contain}]

Step 5. This is the Bayes-optimal decision rule for the given loss function.

\boxed{\text{The blocking rule minimizes expected loss.} \quad \blacksquare}

