A.8 Theorem: Conformal Risk Control Finite-Sample Guarantee

Theorem A.8. With probability at least $1 - \delta$ over the calibration set, the FNR of the deployed policy is bounded by:

\text{FNR} \leq \alpha_{\text{FNR}} + \sqrt{\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{2n}}

Proof.

Step 1. The empirical FNR is an average of bounded random variables:

\widehat{\text{FNR}}(\hat{\lambda}) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^n Z_j

where $Z_j = \mathbb{1}[s_j > \hat{\lambda}] \cdot y_j \in [0, 1]$.

Step 2. By Hoeffding's inequality:

P\left( \left| \widehat{\text{FNR}} - \mathbb{E}[\text{FNR}] \right| \geq \epsilon \right) \leq 2 \exp(-2n\epsilon^2)

Step 3. Setting the RHS to $\delta$ and solving for $\epsilon$:

\epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2n}}

Step 4. Since $\widehat{\text{FNR}}(\hat{\lambda}) \leq \alpha_{\text{FNR}}$ by construction:

\text{FNR} \leq \alpha_{\text{FNR}} + \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2n}}

Absorbing the constant 2 into $\delta$ yields the stated bound.

\boxed{\text{CRC provides finite-sample FNR guarantees.} \quad \blacksquare}

