A.9 Theorem: Bounded Model Uncertainty Preserves Safety

Theorem A.9. If the robust safety risk $R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t)$ satisfies $R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t) \leq R_{\max}$ and the decision-theoretic blocking rule is applied, then the maximum harm $H_i(t)$ is bounded by $H_{\max}$.

Proof.

Step 1. By definition, $R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t) = \sup_{Q \in \mathcal{C}_\alpha} \mathbb{E}_Q[P(\text{violation} \mid a, c, h)]$.

Step 2. If $R_i^{\text{safety, robust}}(t) \leq R_{\max}$, then for all $Q \in \mathcal{C}_\alpha$, the violation probability is bounded by $R_{\max}$.

Step 3. The harm $H_i(t)$ is bounded by the maximum consequence times the violation probability:

H_i(t) \leq \text{MaxConsequence}(\mathcal{A}_i) \cdot R_{\max}

Step 4. By the authority envelope bound (§2):

\text{MaxConsequence}(\mathcal{A}_i) \leq f(P_i(t)) + \epsilon_{\text{unknown}}

Step 5. Combining:

H_i(t) \leq (f(P_i(t)) + \epsilon_{\text{unknown}}) \cdot R_{\max}

Step 6. Setting $H_{\max} = (f(P_i(t)) + \epsilon_{\text{unknown}}) \cdot R_{\max}$ yields the bound.

\boxed{\text{Bounded robust risk implies bounded harm.} \quad \blacksquare}

