A.10 Theorem: Root-of-Trust Independence Prevents Correlated Compromise

Theorem A.10. If the root-of-trust independence score satisfies $\text{RTI}(\mathcal{G}) \geq 1$, then a single supply-chain compromise cannot compromise the quorum.

Proof.

Step 1. Suppose a single vendor $V^*$ is compromised.

Step 2. By RTI ≥ 1, the quorum contains at least $f+1$ distinct vendors. At most one is $V^*$.

Step 3. Therefore, at least $f$ guardians use non-compromised vendors.

Step 4. For the quorum to be compromised, an attacker needs $f+1$ Byzantine guardians (to break the $2f+1$ honest majority).

Step 5. With only one vendor compromised, the attacker can corrupt at most the guardians using $V^*$, which is fewer than $f+1$.

Step 6. The quorum remains safe.

\boxed{\text{Single-vendor compromise cannot break the quorum.} \quad \blacksquare}

