PART II — DETAILED SPECIFICATIONS

Appendix B — Conformal Risk Control Algorithm

B.1 Problem Statement

Given a calibration set of traces $\{(a_j, c_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^n$ where $y_j \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates whether the action was a violation, we seek a threshold $\hat{\lambda}$ such that the false-negative rate (FNR) is bounded by $\alpha_{\text{FNR}}$.

B.2 Algorithm

Input: Calibration set $\{(a_j, c_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^n$, target FNR $\alpha_{\text{FNR}}$, model $\hat{P}(\text{violation} \mid a, c)$.

Output: Threshold $\hat{\lambda}$.

```
1. For each j, compute nonconformity score:
   s_j = 1 - \hat{P}(\text{violation} \mid a_j, c_j)

2. Sort scores: s_{(1)} \leq s_{(2)} \leq \dots \leq s_{(n)}

3. For each candidate threshold \lambda in sorted scores:
   Compute empirical FNR:
   \widehat{FNR}(\lambda) = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^n \mathbb{1}[s_j > \lambda] \cdot y_j}{\sum_{j=1}^n y_j}

4. Choose \hat{\lambda} = \min \{ \lambda : \widehat{FNR}(\lambda) \leq \alpha_{\text{FNR}} \}

5. Block if \hat{P}(\text{violation} \mid a, c) > 1 - \hat{\lambda}
```

B.3 Dual Credible Sets

The oracle must output two credible sets:

· $\mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{alone}}$: credible set when the agent acts alone
· $\mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{under\_peer\_pressure}}$: credible set when the agent observes peers

Blocking rule:

\texttt{BLOCK} \iff \sup_{Q \in \mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{alone}}} \mathbb{E}_Q[\text{violation}] > \tau \;\; \vee \;\; \sup_{Q \in \mathcal{C}_\alpha^{\text{under\_peer\_pressure}}} \mathbb{E}_Q[\text{violation}] > \tau

B.4 Credible Set Construction

Alone credible set: Constructed from the posterior $P(h \mid \text{history})$ with no peer observations.

Under-peer-pressure credible set: Constructed from the posterior $P(h \mid \text{history}, \text{peer\_signals})$ where peer signals are the observed actions of neighboring agents.

The under-peer-pressure credible set is wider to account for conformity effects.

B.5 Calibration Maintenance

Calibration must be re-run when:

· The deployment distribution shifts (detected by drift monitoring)
· New agent capabilities are introduced
· New threat classes are identified
· The ACB benchmark is updated

Re-calibration frequency: at least every 30 days or after any distribution shift event.

